\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Normalized Haar measure}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Every compact Hausdorff topological group #{G} has a unique
regular Borel probability measure #{\mu_G} invariant under left
translation. This is the normalized Haar theorem, used here as a
\em{cited statement}; existence and uniqueness are not proved in
these notes. The existence and uniqueness-up-to-scale statements are
Theorems 4.3 and 4.6 in
\citet{pp. 6, 11}{gleason2010haar}. Compactness permits normalization
to total mass one.}

\p{The normalized measure is also invariant under right translation
and inversion. Indeed, a right translate of #{\mu_G} is another
left-invariant regular probability measure, hence equals #{\mu_G}
by uniqueness. The pushforward under inversion is then left invariant,
because inversion changes left translations into right translations;
it too has total mass one and equals #{\mu_G}. These deductions
explain why the same measure works with either orientation of an edge.}

\p{Every nonempty open set has positive Haar measure. If an open
set had measure zero, choose a point in it; its left translates cover
#{G}, and compactness gives a finite subcover, contradicting total
mass one. Consequently a continuous function that vanishes
#{\mu_G}-almost everywhere vanishes everywhere.}

\p{For a finite group this measure is uniform counting. For
#{U(1)=\{\ue^{\ui\theta}:\theta\in\mathbb R\}}, it is
#{\frac{\extd\theta}{2\pi}} on one period: translation invariance and total
mass one identify this measure by uniqueness. Product Haar measure
on the edge space #{G^E} supplies the compact measure assumed in the
\vocabk{joint matter law}{fdip-0076}. For #{U(1)} and #{SU(2)}, all spaces
here are compact metrizable, so their Borel products have the usual
product measurable structure.}

\p{Mathlib constructs Haar measure as
\leanref{MeasureTheory.Measure.haarMeasure}, normalized on a chosen
compact set with nonempty interior; for compact #{G}, choose that
set to be all of #{G}. The separate declaration
\leanref{MeasureTheory.Measure.isHaarMeasure_eq_of_isProbabilityMeasure}
asserts equality of two Haar probability measures on a locally compact
topological group with its Borel measurable structure. It has no
second-countability assumption. These are correspondences for the
construction and normalized uniqueness, respectively; the right and
inversion invariance arguments above are stated separately.}
